# Deterministic line endings across Windows/Linux/macOS contributors.
#
# Without this, normalization depends on each contributor's `core.autocrlf`,
# so whether a file lands in the repository as LF or CRLF is a property of
# whoever happened to commit it. Two concrete failures follow from that:
#
#   * A shell script committed with CRLF is unrunnable on Linux and macOS -
#     the interpreter line becomes `#!/bin/sh\r` and `curl ... | sh` dies with
#     a "bad interpreter" error that names a file that plainly exists.
#   * A detached signature or other byte-exact blob committed through a text
#     filter is silently corrupted, and the corruption only surfaces at verify
#     time, long after the commit that caused it.
#
# Store everything as LF in the repository and check it out as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit text types (belt-and-suspenders over the auto detection).
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.wxs   text eol=lf
*.xsl   text eol=lf

# Binary assets - never normalize or diff as text.
*.ttf      binary
*.otf      binary
*.woff     binary
*.woff2    binary
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.gif      binary
*.ico      binary
*.icns     binary
*.gz       binary
*.zip      binary
*.wasm     binary
*.minisig  binary

# Why the `*.sh` (and `*.yml`/`*.yaml`) rules above are load-bearing rather
# than cosmetic.
#
# `packaging/require-installer-signing.sh` is executed by the windows-installer
# job, on windows-latest, via `shell: bash`. A CRLF checkout there does not
# merely look untidy: the trailing \r becomes part of the last token on every
# line, so `sh` reports `$'\r': command not found`, and — worse, because it
# fails QUIETLY in the wrong direction — a comparison like
#
#     [ "${UNSIGNED_WINDOWS_INSTALLER_ACK:-}" = "${ACK_SENTINEL}" ]
#
# compares against a sentinel with a trailing \r and can never match, silently
# turning the acknowledged path back into a hard failure. Whether that happens
# depends on the runner image's core.autocrlf, which is not ours to control.
# Declaring the endings above removes the dependency entirely. The workflows
# carry embedded shell in `run:` blocks that runs on Linux and macOS runners,
# so they are pinned for the same reason.
